; The project verdict aliases. They live at the root rather than in a
; test/ directory because this repo has no single test tree — every
; package carries its own (matrix/test, mosaic/test/unit, toffee/test,
; …) — and these three answer questions about the project as a whole.
;
; cover and mutate are asymmetric on purpose. Coverage accumulates as a
; side effect of any instrumented run, so @cover both runs the suites
; and merges. A mutation verdict only exists if a run was asked to test
; mutants, so @mutate merges what previous runs left:
;
;   WINDTRAP_MUTATE=1 dune runtest --force \
;     --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.mutate
;   dune build @mutate
;
; (universe) is load-bearing in both: the .coverage and .mutants files
; are written by test executables at exit and are not declarable
; dependencies, so it makes the milliseconds-cheap merge re-run on every
; build.
;
; --min 68 is the measured baseline (70.55% over 30,647 points, merged
; across every package) minus a little headroom. It ratchets: raise it
; when the margin is comfortable, never lower it to make a red build
; green.

(rule
 (alias cover)
 (deps
  (alias_rec runtest)
  (universe))
 (action
  (chdir
   %{workspace_root}
   (run %{bin:windtrap} coverage --min 68))))

(rule
 (alias mutate)
 (deps (universe))
 (action
  (chdir
   %{workspace_root}
   (run %{bin:windtrap} mutate))))

; @admit asks "can every test this run executes fail?" and answers with
; its exit code, so it is only useful while it is green — a red one
; teaches that red is normal, which is the opposite of what UNJUSTIFIED
; means. It is scoped for a second reason too: an admission run tries up
; to 25 faults per test, so aiming it at all ~4,400 tests would take
; hours and nobody would run it.
;
; test_selection (24 admitted, 5 no sites) and test_edit_buffer (140
; admitted) both audit clean, so @admit runs both. Add an executable here
; once its own audit is clean.
;
; Two suites are known NOT to be clean yet, and the numbers are worth
; keeping where someone will find them rather than in a review comment:
;
;   WINDTRAP_MUTATE=audit dune exec --instrument-with ppx_windtrap.mutate \
;     mosaic/test/unit/test_text_buffer.exe
;     -> 42 admitted, 9 unjustified, 3 no sites
;   ... test_event.exe -> 7 admitted, 1 unjustified, 30 no sites
;
; test_edit_buffer reached 140/140 from 135/5, and how is worth keeping.
; Its five UNJUSTIFIED were read as two equivalent mutants; only
; edit_buffer.ml:23 `max_graphemes <= 0` was one, and it carries a
; dismissal now. The rest were weak tests, weak in one particular way:
; each observed nothing but an empty buffer, and every fault the
; construction path reaches is the identity on empty content. Sharper
; assertions on the same three numbers could not have helped. Observing
; more of the object did — four of the five now walk find_line's binary
; search or a real grapheme run and kill a fault there.
;
; One finding was not mosaic's at all. matrix_text.ml:12 `w <= 0`
; survived matrix's own suite over 91 evaluations because nothing
; asserted that a tab_width of 0 falls back to the default; test_text
; carries that row now. `len = 0` at matrix_text.ml:191 and :204 was the
; near-miss — dismissing it would have suppressed a fault that
; test_text kills outright, since `len <> 0` makes iter_graphemes a
; no-op for every non-empty string. A fault one test cannot observe is
; a weak test, not an equivalent mutant.

(rule
 (alias admit)
 (deps
  (universe)
  mosaic/test/unit/test_selection.exe
  mosaic/test/unit/test_edit_buffer.exe)
 (action
  (setenv
   WINDTRAP_MUTATE
   audit
   (progn
    (run %{exe:mosaic/test/unit/test_selection.exe})
    (run %{exe:mosaic/test/unit/test_edit_buffer.exe})))))
